@page "/docs/tools/cli"

@* The izzy command (src/IzzyBlazor.Cli): install, the commands, and what it writes. Each kind of item has a page
   of its own in this section. Its help text and exit codes are in Cli.cs; keep them in step with these pages.
   Until Nov 16, 2026 the whole CLI was one article at docs/get-started/cli; until Nov 26, 2026 this page was
   docs/cli/overview, the first of a section named "The izzy CLI". *@

<ArticlePage Section="tools" Slug="cli">
    <Lead>
        The <Code>izzy</Code> command creates new apps from <Anchor Href="docs/tools/templates">templates</Anchor>, sets up IzzyBlazor in an app you already have, and adds
        <Anchor Href="blocks">blocks</Anchor> and pages to your app. The code it adds is yours to change. You can see every
        change before izzy makes it.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Install</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy is a .NET tool, and you can run it two ways. An app that izzy created or set up has its own copy. To
            create an app or to set up one you have, install izzy for your whole machine.
        </TextBlock>
        <Heading Level="3">In an app</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy new</Code>, the <Code>dotnet new</Code> templates and <Code>izzy init</Code> each add izzy to
            the app's tool manifest, <Code>.config/dotnet-tools.json</Code>, at the version of the IzzyBlazor package
            the app references. In the app's folder, restore it once, then run it through <Code>dotnet</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@InApp</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            This needs nothing on your PATH, and everyone who clones the app gets the same izzy. Commit the manifest
            with your app. Inside such an app, izzy writes the commands it tells you to run as <Code>dotnet izzy</Code>.
            The rest of these pages write <Code>izzy</Code>: put <Code>dotnet</Code> before it when you use the app's copy.
        </TextBlock>
        <Heading Level="3">On your machine</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet tool install -g IzzyBlazor.Cli --prerelease</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            .NET puts the command in <Code>~/.dotnet/tools</Code> (<Code>%USERPROFILE%\.dotnet\tools</Code> on
            Windows). When the terminal answers "command not found", that folder isn't on your PATH: see
            <Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting/izzy-not-found">izzy: command not found</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Check your setup</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy doctor</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy doctor</Code> checks what izzy needs on your machine and in the app in this folder: the
            .NET SDK that folder uses, the app's IzzyBlazor package and its own copy of izzy against the izzy
            you ran, your account, and the browser for <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit">izzy audit</Anchor>.
            Each line says what it found, and one that is off says how to fix it. <Code>--build</Code> builds
            the app too, and <Code>--browser</Code> names the browser to check. It changes nothing and needs no account. <Code>--json</Code> prints the checks as
            data, each with an <Code>id</Code>, a <Code>level</Code> (ok, info, warn or fail) and what it
            <Code>found</Code>. One that is off has a <Code>fix</Code>, a sentence that says what to do, and
            where there is one, the <Code>command</Code> that does it or the <Code>url</Code> to get it from.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Update</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The CLI has the same version as the library. When a newer release is out, izzy says so in one line after
            a command. It never updates by itself.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            In your app's folder, <Code>izzy update</Code> moves the app to the newest release. It changes the version
            of the IzzyBlazor package in your project files and of izzy in the app's tool manifest, then builds the
            app. Add <Code>--dry-run</Code> or <Code>--diff</Code> to see the change first, or
            <Code>--to</Code> with a version to pick the release.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@UpdateApp</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy update</Code> ends with what the releases in between removed or renamed. Razor takes an old
            parameter name without a warning, and the page fails when it opens, so read the list.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy check</Code> lists where your app still uses them, by file, line and column, with what
            replaces each. It looks in the <Code>.razor</Code>, <Code>.cs</Code> and <Code>.css</Code> files, so a
            renamed CSS variable shows up too. Add <Code>--to</Code> with a version to see what moving to it would break.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            With IzzyBlazor Pro, <Code>izzy upgrade</Code> makes the changes. It prints a plan and writes nothing: each rename it
            would make, and what is left to change by hand, by file and line with what to use instead. Add
            <Code>--diff</Code> to see the renames as a diff. When the plan is right,
            <Code>izzy upgrade --apply</Code> makes the renames. It starts from the release <Code>izzy update</Code> moved your app from, kept in
            <Code>.izzy/upgrade.json</Code> until nothing is left to change. For an app you updated by hand, name
            that release with <Code>--from</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@UpgradeApp</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Outside an app, or with <Code>--global</Code>, <Code>izzy update</Code> updates the izzy on your machine.
            It runs <Code>dotnet tool update -g IzzyBlazor.Cli</Code> with the version.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To learn of a release, izzy asks nuget.org for the package's versions, at most once a day, while a
            command runs. The request doesn't wait for an answer after the command ends, and it says nothing about
            you or your app. izzy doesn't ask in CI, with <Code>--json</Code>, or when a script or an editor reads its
            output. To turn it off, set <Code>IZZY_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</Code>. <Code>izzy update</Code> itself asks
            each time, unless you give it <Code>--to</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Commands</Heading>
        <Table Items="Commands" Caption="Commands" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Command" Width="Length.Rem(14)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Run <Code>izzy add</Code> in your app's folder. To see what you can add, run <Code>izzy list</Code>. To see an
            item's settings, run <Code>izzy describe</Code> with its kind and id, such as <Code>izzy describe block hero</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The code is yours</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy adds plain Razor and C# files to your app. Nothing is hidden in a package, so you can change the text,
            the layout and the code. To see what you can do with it, read the <Anchor Href="docs/tools/license">License</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy doesn't replace a file unless you add <Code>--overwrite</Code>. To see what a command would change, add
            <Code>--diff</Code>. See <Anchor Href="docs/tools/changes">Previewing changes</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy records each item it adds in <Code>.izzy/items.json</Code>, with its version and settings. Commit this file
            with your app.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/tools/account">Your account</Anchor>: sign in for the app starters, blocks and kits.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/tools/templates">Templates</Anchor>: create an app from a starter or an app kit.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/tools/blocks">Blocks</Anchor>: add a hero, pricing or a sign-up form to a page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/tools/pages">Pages and components</Anchor>: add a new page or a component with no route.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/tools/crud">Pages for a Model</Anchor>: create a page to display, add, edit and delete a model's records.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit">Accessibility audit</Anchor>: check a page with axe-core and get a report.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/tools/changes">Previewing changes</Anchor>: see a change before izzy makes it.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/tools/ai-assistants">AI assistants</Anchor>: set up your assistant with <Code>izzy agent</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/tools/license">License</Anchor>: what you can do with the code izzy creates.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string InApp = """
        dotnet tool restore
        dotnet izzy list
        """;

    private const string UpdateApp = """
        izzy update --diff
        izzy update
        """;

    private const string UpgradeApp = """
        izzy upgrade
        izzy upgrade --apply
        izzy upgrade --from 0.1.0-alpha.20
        """;

    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] Commands =
    [
        new("izzy new starter|kit <id> -n <name>", "Creates a new app from a starter or an app kit, as dotnet new does, such as izzy new starter sidebar or izzy new kit crm. See Create an app and Templates."),
        new("izzy init", "Sets up IzzyBlazor in an app you already have. See Add to an existing app."),
        new("izzy update", "Moves your app to the newest IzzyBlazor: the package and the app's izzy. Outside an app, it updates the izzy on your machine. See Update."),
        new("izzy check", "Lists where your app uses what a release renamed or removed, CSS variables too, with what replaces each. See Update."),
        new("izzy upgrade", "Plans the changes for what the releases between your app's old version and the new one removed or renamed. With --apply, renames what has a new name (Pro). See Update."),
        new("izzy add block <id>", "Adds a block to your app, such as izzy add block hero."),
        new("izzy add page --name <Name>", "Adds a new page with its route and title, and with --nav a link in the app's nav. See Pages and components."),
        new("izzy add razor --name <Name>", "Adds a component with no route, and the @using its folder needs. See Pages and components."),
        new("izzy add component <Name> --at <file>:<line>", "Adds a component's usage example from these docs to a page. See Components."),
        new("izzy add crud <Model>", "Creates a page to display, add, edit and delete a model's records."),
        new("izzy wrap, unwrap, extract", "Puts a component around markup, takes one away, or moves markup to a component of its own. A free account. See Wrap, unwrap and extract."),
        new("izzy doctor", "Checks the .NET SDK, your app's versions, your account and the audit's browser, and says how to fix what is off."),
        new("izzy login", "Signs this machine in to your free IzzyBlazor account, which the app starters, blocks and kits come with. See Your account."),
        new("izzy audit <url>", "Checks a page's accessibility with axe-core in the Chrome or Edge on your machine, and writes a report. See Accessibility audit."),
        new("izzy agent", "Sets up your AI assistant in your app. It writes a skill that teaches the assistant to build with IzzyBlazor, and connects the docs server and izzy mcp. See AI assistants."),
        new("izzy mcp", "Runs izzy's commands as tools for your AI assistant. izzy agent adds it to your assistant's settings."),
        new("izzy list", "Lists the starters, kits, blocks and components. To list one kind, name it, such as izzy list block or izzy list component."),
        new("izzy describe starter|kit|block <id>", "Lists an item's settings, such as izzy describe block hero. With --json, it also lists the components the item uses, how it works in each render mode, and its accessibility notes."),
        new("izzy describe component <Name>", "Lists a component's options, content, events and examples, such as izzy describe component DataGrid. It describes your app's version of the library."),
        new("izzy describe tokens", "Lists the library's CSS variables by group, with their light and dark values and what each is for. With --json, it also lists the internal ones, the old names releases renamed, and IzzyTheme's settings. See Tokens."),
        new("izzy add token <name>", "Adds a color of your own, such as izzy add token promo --light #7c3aed: its variables in wwwroot/theme.css and its Accent and Color members. izzy rename token and izzy remove token change it everywhere your app has it. See Tokens."),
        new("izzy capabilities --json", "Lists this izzy's commands, their options and the plan each needs, for an editor or agent that drives izzy."),
        new("izzy theme vars", "Writes what IzzyTheme puts in the page's head for the settings you give, such as izzy theme vars --brand \"#0f766e\" --radius full."),
        new("izzy theme sample", "Writes a page that shows a theme on the library's components, for the settings izzy theme vars takes, with no app running."),
    ];
}
